# Line endings, pinned.
#
# This tree gets worked on from Windows and from WSL against the *same*
# directory under /mnt/c, by two git installations that do not share a global
# config. Without this file each of them applies its own `core.autocrlf`: the
# Git for Windows install has it on and normalises CRLF away on read, while
# WSL's git 2.34 leaves it unset. Same bytes, two verdicts — WSL reported 231
# files modified with 81763 insertions and 81763 deletions, all of it carriage
# returns (2026-08-17). Real edits are invisible in that noise, and `git
# rebase` refuses to start at all.
#
# eol=lf rather than crlf because LF is what the repository already stores and
# what the tools need: a .sh checked out with CRLF cannot run under WSL at all
# (`bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`), which is what scripts/hw_check.sh had become.
# Rust, TypeScript, TOML, JSON and Markdown are indifferent, and PowerShell
# reads LF happily.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the exception: cmd.exe is the one consumer here that is
# genuinely happier with CRLF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never let the heuristic guess at these. It gets them right today, but a
# mis-detected binary that goes through eol conversion is corrupted silently.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
